@@ -0,0 +1,82 @@
+# ADR 0162 — Page access: read and write per page, bulk-managed
+
+Date: 2026-10-01
+Status: Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+Access was a per-section level (`none`, `viewer`, `editor` across Dashboard,
+Demand, Supply, Reporting and Agents) plus one `reporting.view.*` capability per
+report and a short list of high-risk capabilities. A section level could not
+separate two pages in the same section, most Demand and Supply pages had no
+read guard at all (only their actions checked the level), and the Access page
+edited one person at a time with three groups of controls, so nobody could see
+who held what or give ten people the same access.
+
+The user asked (2026-10-01) for permissions "by page and where relevant read or
+write permissions for interactional pages where write is read and write", and
+for an Access page that manages many people at once and shows who has what.
+
+## Decision
+
+- **A page grant is a capability.** `<page id>:read` and `<page id>:write`,
+  with the page ids from the route table (ADR 0160), live in
+  `capability_grants` next to the extra permissions. `requireCapability`,
+  `requireCapabilityPage` and `can()` already guard every action, page and
+  route, so no new guard function exists. That also keeps the gate policy
+  (`scripts/gate-policy.json`) unchanged: the baseline ratchet counts a new
+  guard name as a looser gate.
+- **One catalog.** `CATALOG` in `lib/permissions.ts` gives each gated page a
+  mode: `view` (none or read), `edit` (none, read, write; write includes read)
+  or `actions` (open only to holders of its extra permissions, such as Edit POs
+  and Deletions). `Capability` is derived from it, so a typo fails the build,
+  and `lib/permissions.test.ts` fails until every route table page is in the
+  catalog or listed as open. A page offers `write` only where it has something
+  to change.
+- **Extra permissions stay separate.** The thirteen `ActionKey`s are higher
+  risk than an ordinary edit (submit a PO, approve a plan deletion, manage
+  users) and each belongs to one page, shown under it on the Access page.
+- **Actions shared by two pages accept either.** The three supply grid pages
+  share their actions and modals, so those guards pass `SUPPLY_GRID_READ` or
+  `SUPPLY_GRID_WRITE`; the Variant and Channels pages share the demand plan
+  mutations and the Variant and Placeholder Items pages the placeholder ones.
+- **No landing loop.** `requireCapabilityPage` redirects a user without the
+  grant to the first page they can open (`landingPath`), or to `/not-invited`
+  when there is none. The top nav shows a tab only when the user can open a
+  page in it, and links it to that page.
+- **The Access page is a bulk editor.** Select one or many people; every control
+  applies to everyone selected and shows "Mixed" where they differ. Templates
+  replace page access (and clear extra permissions, which no template grants),
+  "Copy from" copies another person's access, every change returns an Undo, and
+  a matrix shows who holds what. Server actions
+  (`settings/access/actions.ts`) write the net change in one transaction and
+  refuse to let someone remove their own `users.manage`. Only admins can
+  remove an admin; admins cannot be edited because they hold everything.
+- **Migration 0036 converts, then deletes the retired rows.** Section levels
+  become page grants (viewer: read; editor: write where the page can change),
+  each `reporting.view.X` becomes the page's `:read`, and `*.manage` plus the
+  page's view grant becomes `:write`. Wholesale Comp and Lead Time Exceptions
+  write needs the old section editor level as well as the view grant, as their
+  actions required. New Variant Demand writes were open to Supply viewers, so
+  viewers keep write there. A person with no `section_access` rows gets no
+  page grants, so a member who could open Demand and Supply pages only because
+  they had no read guard now lands on `/not-invited` until given access. The
+  app parses every stored capability and fails on an unknown one, so the
+  migration deletes the `reporting.view.*`, `reporting.items.manage` and
+  `reporting.order_reservations.manage` rows in the same transaction.
+  `section_access` is left in place and unread. Code from before this change
+  fails on the new capability rows, so rolling the app back past it means
+  restoring the database too. `drizzle-kit push` on a local database offers to
+  drop `section_access`, which is safe to accept.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Wholesale Comp planner notes, Export EOM and plan snapshots now follow page
+  levels: notes need write on Wholesale Comp, the demand plan snapshot needs
+  write on Variant.
+- The Demand, Supply and Agent settings pages are catalog pages (`demand.settings`,
+  `supply.settings`) or open (`agents.settings` stays behind `settings.manage`).
+- GET routes that only check "signed in" were not changed.
+- Access changes are not audited yet. Undo restores a person's whole grant set
+  from the snapshot taken before the change, so it overwrites an edit another
+  admin made to that person in between.
